WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
(calming music)

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.903
Hi guys my name is Heinrich and

00:00:07.903 --> 00:00:11.340
welcome to another coffee
break with firm learning.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.710
Today the weather is beautiful in Munich,

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.860
we had a nice brunch this morning

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.510
together with a good friend of mine,

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.320
who is actually an entrepreneur,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.060
he has a small company here in Munich

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.070
employing about forty people

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.890
and we talked about a new
employee in his company

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:29.810
that's a new guy he just left university

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:30.840
he has good grades,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.080
seems to be quite good and confident

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.370
and actually being able
to do the jobs well.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.940
However, for some reason
my friend doesn't seem

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.653
to be just satisfied with
what he is doing at work,

00:00:42.653 --> 00:00:44.760
and we talked a bit about it

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.780
and what the reason might be

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.910
because it wasn't directly easy to grasp

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.930
and after some time we
came to the realization

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.780
that what this person was actually missing

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.910
was an ownership mindset.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:00.370
So you may ask yourself what
is an ownership mindset?

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.040
An ownership mindset is a critical mindset

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.391
that on the one hand side

00:01:04.391 --> 00:01:05.224
many new joiners and business lack,

00:01:05.224 --> 00:01:08.530
many new joiners and business lack,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.215
if you come from university
often you do not have that

00:01:11.215 --> 00:01:14.772
but on the other side this
is really what distinguishes

00:01:14.772 --> 00:01:17.650
the average guy, the average professional

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.640
from somebody who really stands out

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.090
and is perceived as much better

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.720
than all of the other people in the team.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.052
So an ownership mindset is if

00:01:25.052 --> 00:01:27.950
you receive a task from your team leader

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:31.860
or another colleague and you
do not only try to somehow

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.967
get the task done but you
feel completely responsible

00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:39.028
from end to end to getting to the result

00:01:39.028 --> 00:01:39.861
from end to end to getting to the result

00:01:39.861 --> 00:01:41.074
that is really needed and

00:01:41.074 --> 00:01:44.249
why is that maybe a bit
difficult for new joiners?

00:01:44.249 --> 00:01:46.950
Because of course in the
beginning of your career

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.779
if you just started your
job in the early months

00:01:49.779 --> 00:01:51.960
and maybe first one, two or three years

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.230
of course you are new,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.106
you do not have all the
knowledge of the organization,

00:01:56.106 --> 00:01:59.640
maybe you do not completely
understand everything,

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.487
so you to a certain extent
rely on your other colleagues,

00:02:03.487 --> 00:02:06.139
you rely on others checking your work,

00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:08.590
making sure that your work actually

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.174
has the quality that is needed

00:02:10.174 --> 00:02:13.847
and that it just is the way
it is supposed to be right?

00:02:13.847 --> 00:02:17.600
And of course to a certain
extent that's okay.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.210
It's completely natural and
everybody will understand

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.075
that as a new employee you do not

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:24.118
know everything from the beginning,

00:02:24.118 --> 00:02:26.170
you might even make mistakes,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.600
maybe the quality of
your work is not as good

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:32.420
as maybe the work from
a more senior colleague.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.760
However, the flip side of that is that

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.368
people start to rely
and to depend on that,

00:02:37.368 --> 00:02:39.680
what I mean by that is that people

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.553
start to develop thoughts such as,

00:02:41.553 --> 00:02:46.467
"Well my team leaders
reviewing after what's anyway",

00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:48.590
"Well if my team leader has it and

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:49.793
he wants to change something,

00:02:49.793 --> 00:02:52.170
well he can still do that, right?"

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.640
Or, "Well this is just a first draft,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.460
I mean its just a first try for me,

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.489
let the others that maybe will refine it",

00:02:59.489 --> 00:03:02.233
or, "After myself many other people

00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:04.130
will still review the document,

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:04.963
will still have a look at it

00:03:04.963 --> 00:03:06.992
and if there is anything wrong with that

00:03:06.992 --> 00:03:08.876
then they will fix it, right?"

00:03:08.876 --> 00:03:12.210
I mean this is more or less
consciously the mindset

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.931
that many people have,

00:03:13.931 --> 00:03:17.160
especially in the
beginning of their career.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.420
And the truth is that
many people do their work

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.120
knowingly that it's just not enough,

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.983
knowingly that the end
deliverable that they created

00:03:26.983 --> 00:03:29.238
is just not meeting the standards

00:03:29.238 --> 00:03:33.029
and just relying or just
assuming that other people

00:03:33.029 --> 00:03:34.246
will pick up the slack,

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:36.330
that your team lead will then actually

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.360
give it a proper review and
then fix all the errors,

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.080
maybe change it to a
way and to a level that

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.632
is actually enough and sufficient for

00:03:43.632 --> 00:03:45.640
actually what's required.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.130
And again from the
perspective of your team lead,

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.700
while on the one side you of
course understand that maybe

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.242
a new joiner isn't as good as
somebody who is more senior,

00:03:53.242 --> 00:03:54.220
of course this is annoying and

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.660
of course this is annoying and

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.800
is really bugging your time, right?

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.730
And from my experience now
from working in consulting

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:03.794
what of course happens sometimes is that

00:04:03.794 --> 00:04:05.530
you will trust another colleague

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.046
maybe a support colleague of
yours to get something done,

00:04:08.046 --> 00:04:10.840
to do something and then
you receive the deliverable

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.126
and it's just obvious that this

00:04:13.126 --> 00:04:15.960
is not client ready deliverable, right?

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.762
Its just obvious that other person thought

00:04:17.762 --> 00:04:20.792
"Well, you know Heinrich in
the end is going to fix it,

00:04:20.792 --> 00:04:23.540
or the other consulting
colleague is going to fix it,

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:24.815
and it's going to do all the

00:04:24.815 --> 00:04:26.512
last changes that he maybe needs".

00:04:26.512 --> 00:04:29.760
So it's clear then the moment
that the person created it,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.713
it just wasn't done and
the person know right,

00:04:32.713 --> 00:04:35.048
that it wasn't done and this
is something that you should

00:04:35.048 --> 00:04:37.502
really avoid as a new joiner.

00:04:37.502 --> 00:04:40.260
From very early on in
your career you need to

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.800
develop the mindset
that it's not only about

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.680
getting the to do's off your table,

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.558
right, pushing it out and
just somehow completing them,

00:04:46.558 --> 00:04:49.459
but to make sure that
every little thing you do

00:04:49.459 --> 00:04:51.853
is actually at the level
that it's complete,

00:04:51.853 --> 00:04:53.643
that actually it wouldn't require

00:04:53.643 --> 00:04:56.250
anybody else in your
team and organization,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.632
neither colleague or your
team lead to do any changes

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:01.292
in order for it to be at a level

00:05:01.292 --> 00:05:05.050
where it's at least
sufficient and okay, right?

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.720
Of course there are different perspectives

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.810
maybe you another colleague
has another opinion

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.471
and would like to change
something in it a bit,

00:05:10.471 --> 00:05:12.490
but it should always be at the level

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:14.839
where you would feel
comfortable in actually handing

00:05:14.839 --> 00:05:16.622
the deliverable to a client

00:05:16.622 --> 00:05:17.732
or to an external party

00:05:17.732 --> 00:05:19.689
or somewhere else where this actually

00:05:19.689 --> 00:05:21.780
might be needed in the future.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.200
An easy little mindset hack that I learnt

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.620
from my time at Mackenzie
that you can employ here

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.831
and that are taught to
early young consultants,

00:05:28.831 --> 00:05:32.240
is that you should ask
yourself the following question

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.743
whenever you complete a
deliverable, a certain task,

00:05:34.743 --> 00:05:37.045
a document or whatever you are working on,

00:05:37.045 --> 00:05:40.289
are you confident that you
could send this document

00:05:40.289 --> 00:05:42.930
to a board member of your client

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.769
and he would be okay by reading this

00:05:45.769 --> 00:05:47.951
and nobody would lose face?

00:05:47.951 --> 00:05:50.233
Are you confident that your work

00:05:50.233 --> 00:05:53.660
is ready to being sent to a board member

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:57.060
of your organization
with your name under it?

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.130
If you are not confident
that your work deliverable

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:04.020
is at this level of quality
then very likely you just

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.650
haven't done yet what is required from you

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.750
and should go back and do that.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:10.290
Of course it is great to have

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:12.200
other colleagues check your work,

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.040
to have a four eye principle,

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.052
to make sure that no mistake is left

00:06:16.052 --> 00:06:19.520
or anything like that
but do not rely on them,

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.068
do not rely on other colleagues
getting your work done,

00:06:23.068 --> 00:06:24.482
you are responsible for it,

00:06:24.482 --> 00:06:26.040
you will deliver it,

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:28.480
and you will bring it to a quality that

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.440
you can send it to a board
member of your organization.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.433
So what specifically can you do to develop

00:06:33.433 --> 00:06:35.083
this ownership mindset and make sure that

00:06:35.083 --> 00:06:38.690
your work quality is always
where it needs to be?

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:40.882
So just a couple of tips that I hope

00:06:40.882 --> 00:06:42.520
are helpful to you right?

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.430
That the first thing is if
you find out that you actually

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.510
do not have information at hand
for you to complete the task

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.520
this of course happens frequently,

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:51.706
maybe you need other data,

00:06:51.706 --> 00:06:53.427
input from other colleagues, right?

00:06:53.427 --> 00:06:56.490
Don't just push over it
back to your team lead and

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.568
tell them, "Sorry I couldn't
complete it because I just

00:06:58.568 --> 00:07:00.671
didn't have all the
information that I needed".

00:07:00.671 --> 00:07:03.363
But reach out yourself to
these other colleagues,

00:07:03.363 --> 00:07:04.298
to these other departments,

00:07:04.298 --> 00:07:07.157
do some research, collect
everything you need

00:07:07.157 --> 00:07:08.990
and then go back to your team lead

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:11.350
and tell them, "Well in
the beginning this wasn't

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.140
sufficient but I made
sure that I have all these

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.360
other inputs and now
this is the deliverable

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.820
that you really need".

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:19.500
Another example, imagine your
team lead came to you and

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.570
told you, you need to
create a certain document,

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.020
a certain thing and by
that you should include,

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.808
three certain graphs for instance,

00:07:26.808 --> 00:07:29.970
three certain charts,
with a data visualization

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:31.890
that he felt like this is what's required

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:34.378
and helpful here but by
now crunching the numbers,

00:07:34.378 --> 00:07:36.161
by digging into the work,

00:07:36.161 --> 00:07:38.850
you find out that actually
these three things

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.037
that he asked you to
do are just not enough

00:07:41.037 --> 00:07:43.170
and to actually understand
what is going on

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.493
and to really get the full
grasp of the situation,

00:07:45.493 --> 00:07:47.766
it would just be required to include

00:07:47.766 --> 00:07:51.040
a fourth diagram into the piece of work

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:52.134
that you are working on, right?

00:07:52.134 --> 00:07:53.943
Now what many new joiners do is,

00:07:53.943 --> 00:07:56.486
they just do exactly what they are told,

00:07:56.486 --> 00:07:57.773
they create the document,

00:07:57.773 --> 00:08:01.770
include the three diagrams
hand it back to the team lead

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.320
or the boss or whatever it's
called in your organization

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:05.153
and then they are like,

00:08:05.153 --> 00:08:07.357
"Well, I did everything what
was asked of me," Right?

00:08:07.357 --> 00:08:10.940
And yes of course you
did everything what has

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:12.817
been asked from you but in this instance

00:08:12.817 --> 00:08:14.460
that just wasn't enough,

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:15.940
it just wasn't enough to send it to

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:17.882
a board member of your organization

00:08:17.882 --> 00:08:19.470
because what the board member,

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.270
he just wouldn't have had the full picture

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:23.940
and obviously if your team
lead now is looking at it

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.150
and thinking about this he will likely

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:27.000
come back to you and ask you,

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.608
"Hey come on, you should
include the fourth thing

00:08:29.608 --> 00:08:31.727
because this is just
important to do that,"

00:08:31.727 --> 00:08:33.460
and then of course you can do that,

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:35.375
but it's always much better and perceived

00:08:35.375 --> 00:08:37.884
much more professionally
if you think one step ahead

00:08:37.884 --> 00:08:39.721
and directly include that

00:08:39.721 --> 00:08:42.643
because you know that it's
required to include it

00:08:42.643 --> 00:08:46.153
because nobody usually is
as deep into the details

00:08:46.153 --> 00:08:47.737
of the specifics of the work

00:08:47.737 --> 00:08:51.140
that you're currently
completing than yourself.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.146
Very often in these situations it was just

00:08:53.146 --> 00:08:56.740
not really possible for
your team lead to foresee

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:58.630
that things like that would have needed

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:00.164
to be included because he just wasn't as

00:09:00.164 --> 00:09:02.912
deep as now you are
into the matters, right?

00:09:02.912 --> 00:09:04.239
deep as now you are
into the matters, right?

00:09:04.239 --> 00:09:06.160
So to generalize this a bit more,

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.691
what's really important
is that you ask yourself,

00:09:08.691 --> 00:09:11.483
what is actually the intention of this?

00:09:11.483 --> 00:09:14.310
Why is somebody asking me to do something?

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.986
What is he hoping to get out of that?

00:09:15.986 --> 00:09:18.710
And then you make sure
that the deliverable

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:22.410
that you produce actually
enables the other person

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.830
or whatever the audience
is for the things that

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.406
you are creating, the things
that you are working on,

00:09:26.406 --> 00:09:28.993
to actually answer these questions,

00:09:28.993 --> 00:09:31.570
these problems that they have.

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:33.986
If you're deliverable isn't helpful in

00:09:33.986 --> 00:09:35.480
actually solving the problem,

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.796
or answering these
questions then make sure

00:09:37.796 --> 00:09:40.323
to make all the changes and adjustments

00:09:40.323 --> 00:09:42.560
in order to get there.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.453
Right and of course if
you feel more comfortable

00:09:44.453 --> 00:09:46.477
then go back to your team lead and say,

00:09:46.477 --> 00:09:48.560
"Hey I did one, two,
three but now I feel like

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:49.858
in order for this to be really helpful,

00:09:49.858 --> 00:09:52.500
I think I should do the fourth thing,"

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:54.160
just in case you really want to make sure

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:56.313
that he really agrees as your supervisor

00:09:56.313 --> 00:09:58.526
but be proactive and ask that,

00:09:58.526 --> 00:09:59.900
but be proactive and ask that,

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:02.270
make that recommendation
yourself instead of waiting

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:05.110
for others to tell you what
you already know is required.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:06.810
So guys this is really important,

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:09.380
if you want to be successful
in your early career

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:12.918
but of course also later on
develop an ownership mindset,

00:10:12.918 --> 00:10:16.351
feel responsible for the
tasks that you're working on,

00:10:16.351 --> 00:10:18.600
don't just complete to do's that

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:21.232
somebody else told you
to actually complete

00:10:21.232 --> 00:10:22.799
but you are owning it,

00:10:22.799 --> 00:10:25.080
you are making sure that the things

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.329
you are working on actually
are a great success,

00:10:27.329 --> 00:10:29.700
if you do not adopt
this mindset it will be

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:32.500
extremely difficult for
you to be successful

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.437
in pretty much any organization,

00:10:34.437 --> 00:10:36.500
in any industry you work on.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:37.333
Get that done.

